I'm building [PROJECT NAME] with a team. Here's the workflow I want us to follow:

1. START WITH DOCS, NOT CODE
   - First write README.md (what the project is, architecture overview,
     plain-English explanation then technical terms, repo structure, diagrams where they help)
   - Then SETUP.md (environment setup for Linux/macOS/Windows, exact
     commands, no assumed prior installs)
   - Then a requirements/task-breakdown doc: functional requirements
     (MUST/SHOULD) grouped by feature, followed by a task-by-task
     breakdown (owner, dependencies, steps, acceptance criteria, files
     to create) - this becomes our shared task list

2. BUILD STAGE BY STAGE, ONE TASK AT A TIME
   - I'll tell you which task I'm working on
   - Give me the full code for that task in chat: every file, in order,
     with a short "why it's built this way" after each file - not just
     code with no explanation
   - Tell me which folder/branch it belongs to before we start
   - If a task depends on another unfinished task, say so and suggest
     how to stub around it rather than blocking

3. I RUN THINGS, YOU DIAGNOSE
   - I'll paste real terminal output, including errors
   - Diagnose from the actual output, don't guess or assume a fix
     worked without me confirming
   - If something breaks a downstream step (like local infra state,
     partial writes, or duplicated data), tell me explicitly how to
     undo it cleanly

4. PR DESCRIPTION AFTER EACH TASK
   - Once a task's code is confirmed working, write a PR title and
     description: what it does, file-by-file changes, how it was
     tested (using what I actually confirmed, not assumed), notes for
     reviewers

5. STANDARDS
   - Follow industry standards in writing a Specification.md
   - Plain English in all docs, short sentences, no unnecessary jargon
   - Flag anything inaccurate or overclaimed, including in requirement
     docs, rather than smoothing it over
   - No em dashes in any written document
   - Keep folder structure flat and task-named unless there's a real
     reason to nest something

Confirm you've got this, then ask what the project is and we'll start
with the README.
